A||||39||william_thorniii||?Best Sources for Various Suspension Products?||||5||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||01:48 PM||||Friends,<P>As many of you know, I am in the middle of building a performance suspension for my 86 SE project.  My budget is tight down to the penny and was wondering if some of you have priced some of these components as well.  If you have seen any prices better than those that I quoted PLEASE let me know where/how much.  <P>I am looking at the following components:<P>Wilwood Medium Duty Front Brake Kit ($550)<BR>HAL or Koni externally adjustable shocks <BR>Falken GRB FK-451 tires either 225.40.18 ($181) 215.45.17 ($141)<BR>Advanti Racing 17" ($138) or 18" ($188 - THX Sleeper)<BR>Poly bushings 87 front-end control arm ($33 - THX 2m6)<BR>am I forgetting anything?<P>I already have:<BR>88 rear suspension<BR>Ryane Coil-over and 88 conversion kit<BR>Street Dreams Drop Spindles<P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||10-30-2000||01:53 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.cyberauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cyberauto.com</A>   for stainless braided brake lines... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||02:18 PM||||2m6,<P>Braided lines are one of the things that I have been considering.  Many sources claim that pedal feel will be increased.  However, many other sources claim that the ONLY advantage is resistance to shear from track debris in off-road use.  Does anyone here have any first hand experience?  If so what are your personal reccomendations.  When it comes down to it, they would look pretty trick behind those monster wheels.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>THX again 2m6||||reg||14
Z||000003||Tigger||10-30-2000||02:40 PM||||I think it does both.  Resists being cut by Firestones   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> and also keeps them from expanding like rubber lines to give a more solid brake feel.  They also look cool too.<P>I've been considering those drop spindles on mine.  Any suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||10-30-2000||03:05 PM||||Regular brake lines are steel braided it's just under the rubber coating.||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||03:11 PM||||Tigger,<P>I have not yet installed the spindles.  I received their first pair out the door and have cleaned them up and painted them.  I have removed one old spindle and am going to Auto Zone tonight to get another pickle fork to aid in the removal of my "stubborn" passenger side factory spindle.  They look sharp and significantly stronger though.  Unfortunately I will not be able to form opinions of them for quite a while as my car will not be back on the road until spring (if everything goes well).  I can tell you that Street Dreams is great to deal with.  <P>Phil,<P>Thanks for the info.  I was not aware that rubber lines were steel braided.  All of the lines that I have been exposed to have been cloth braided.  Is there a specific brand that is steel braided, or am I just confused?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000006||Tigger||10-30-2000||03:45 PM||||Thanks WT, that's what I was looking for.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||03:51 PM||||Tigger,<P>When the car is finished I will definitely be heading to the Detroit/Troy.... area.  I will probably run up to Flint to check out the old stompin' grounds as well.  I will drop you a line so we can do a little bit of checking out eachother's tricks.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000008||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||03:52 PM||||How'd that happen?... Double post, sorry<P>William T. Thorn, III<p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 10-30-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||Tigger||10-30-2000||03:56 PM||||Sounds cool.  This winter I'm doing a poly bushing upgrade and was looking into those drop spindles since I'm going to have everything apart.||||reg||1
Z||000010||TheFiero64||10-30-2000||05:07 PM||||I didn't know Wilwood made a brake system that fit on a Fiero. Do you know a Part #? I would love that on my 88.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||10-30-2000||09:54 PM||||WTT3:<P>     you might try rodney dickman's ball joint separator tool instead of a pickle fork. It should save your boots.||||reg||1
Z||000012||WisconsinGT||10-31-2000||12:04 AM||||What are drop spindles? What is there purpose?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||10-31-2000||01:48 AM||||Dropped spindles lower the car without affecting the geometry of the suspension.<BR>It is the best way to lower the front of a car without sacrificing the ride. Also is suppose to be less stress on the suspension as opposed to lowering springs. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||william_thorniii||10-31-2000||07:47 AM||||TheFiero64,<P>The Wilwood brake conversion is not exactly a direct bolt-on swap.  There are some minor modifications that must be made.  The largest of which is that the Fiero 5x100 bolt pattern must be lunched.  I didn't like my wheel selection in the 5x100 size anyway, so I am going for it.  Other than that, the only other thing I need to do is fabricate some sort of bracket to mount the caliper.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000015||TheFiero64||10-31-2000||10:34 AM||||Will, Thanks for the info. Keep us updated on your progress.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Will||10-31-2000||03:04 PM||||What's included in the Wilwood kit? I don't have my Willwod catalog here at school.<BR>     Outlaw &lt;http://www.rebcoracing.com/outlawprod.html&gt; has 12 5/8 x 1 3/8 curved vane rotors for ~$50.00 /ea. Coleman &lt;http://www.colemanracing.com/&gt; has hats  and rotors of various descriptions.<BR>Shop around a little. Wilwood isn't the only manufacturer out there.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phil||10-31-2000||04:54 PM||||It wouldn't stop me but did you know that Wilwood brakes are not DOT approved? Guess they never bothered to get them blessed by the US Gov.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||10-31-2000||07:07 PM||||WTIII (and others)--I have the GA upgrade on all 4 corners with Earls SS braided brake lines.  I can feel a slight difference in pedal feel but this is going from the original (and very worn out) brake lines, etc.  I don't think you would notice much of a difference in the feel if you only changed the brake lines to SS braided.  I'm guessing they're more for looks and abrasion resistance more than anything.||||reg||1
Z||000019||william_thorniii||11-01-2000||01:09 PM||||Will,<P>It depends on who you buy the kit from.  The one that I was quoting included dynalite 4 piston calipers, 11.75" vented rotors, silver anodized aluminum hubs and screw on dust covers. I have tried on a couple of different sources for piecing the kits and it always seems to be a little cheaper to buy it as a bundle (after shipping).  THX for the info though.<P>Phil,<P>I appreciate your concern, but (unless MWBackus is around) I do not worry about gov't mandates etc. unless it is something I am likely to get pulled over for or "should be illegal" in my opinion.  Good luck with your buddies car!<P>Monkeyman,<P>Did you do the SS lines at the same time as the GA brakes?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000020||william_thorniii||11-03-2000||04:35 PM||||Friends,<P>Has anyone bought wheels off of e-bay?  I know, typically Fiero things are very overpriced on e-bay, but some wheels seem to be a good deal.  For example, check out auctions from the user tirepro1, focals are not terrible rims and his prices are down right low.  How ghetto would my lowered ride look with a set of f5's on it?  Although those of you that have been to my 'hood need not comment on how well my car would fit in ghetto'ed :~)<P>Any thoughts?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000021||william_thorniii||11-13-2000||12:34 PM||||Yet another question?<P>Has anyone ordered Chris West's poly transmission and engine mounts?  If so how was the quality?  How was the service?  <P>Are there any other sources out there?  <P>Thanks Again,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000022||1FST2M6||11-13-2000||01:59 PM||||I've installed a set of Chris' mounts.. (not on my car) on A Ga. Fieros club members car. they are SWEET. not sure about the $60 each if it's worth it.. NEW stock ones make a big improvement and don't shake your teeth. and the poly-dog bone is a must. Chris' service is ok...  <P>------------------<BR>"Fondue forks for everybody"||||reg||1
Z||000023||william_thorniii||11-13-2000||02:23 PM||||2m6,<P>Thanks for the input.  They looked impressive, but I too was unsure of the $65 ea (I guess they have gone up $5 ea.)  Let me know when you are working on that TDC swap with mike.  The whole process excites me at a few hundred miles.  I plan on following your install closely to help me in mine in a few years.  <P>Thanks Again,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000024||rockcrawl||11-13-2000||09:30 PM||||I've bought two sets of wheels through ebay, but not from the seller you mentioned. One was a used set from a private seller, and the other was a new set from a dealer somewhere in california. Even with the added cost of shipping, I couldn't even come close to their prices anywhere else.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||14
Z||000025||jhar1987||11-13-2000||11:09 PM||||        <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/85.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Got2manyfieros||11-15-2000||02:13 PM||||The fiero store has a front brake kit that runs about 300 dollars, it isn't wildwood, but one of my associates runs that setup on his racecar (86 GT set up for autocross/roadracing) and he has had excellent results.  The fiero store also offers a high volume master cylender and brake bias distributon block.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com/pegasus/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com/pegasus/index.html</A> <P>-Todd||||reg||1
Z||000027||william_thorniii||11-16-2000||11:42 AM||||Friends,<P>I just found a new source for front and rear sway bars for all years of the Fiero.  PST has sways available with and without bushings and hardware.  It was late last night when I looked through the catalog, but they have one package that includes the bar, all associated hardware and graphite impregnated bushings for $158 front or rear.  The bars are an increased diameter as well (sorry can't remember the specifics).  <P>Just thought this might be of some use to you fierophiles.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000028||william_thorniii||11-21-2000||12:01 PM||||Friends,<P>I recently found these prices on koni's from S F Motorsports in kernersville, NC (336-996-4080).  <P><BR>Front Shocks #8040-1092 @ $74.75, $149.50/pr <BR>Rear Struts #8741-1063 @  $132.25, $264.50/pr <BR>Total $414.00 plus UPS of $11.76, total $425.76<P>Anyone found anything better (Will, lowCG)?  I am interested in manually adjustable damping in rebound.  Thus far would like performance yellow, but would settle for koni reds as well.  <P>William T. Thorn, III <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000029||william_thorniii||11-21-2000||05:36 PM||||Friends,<P>I just got off of the phone with shox.com.  The Koni Specials (Reds) part # 8040-1027 sell for $84 ea for the front and the #8741-1063 sell for $146 ea for the rear.  I need to investigate to determine what the discrepencies in part #'s mean.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>Message Edit:  I am an idiot.  I looked up the numbers for the 88 front above.  The 1027 is the correct shock for the 84-87 front and the 1092 is correct for the 88 front.  The rears are the same for all years.  <p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 11-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Will||11-21-2000||06:59 PM||||I don't know of any better deals on Konis right now, although at this time I'm no in the market.<P>     How are you planning to "lunch" the 5 x 100 bolt circle? I've heard vague things about swapping to the larger bolt circle using Seville hub carriers in the rear, but haven't heard anything about the front.||||reg||1
Z||000031||william_thorniii||11-21-2000||07:44 PM||||Will,<P>That is a good question.  I have considered everything from starlites to custom modifications.  But at this time, I do not have anything definite.  <P>I would really like to be able to modify the stock hub to make it work but have not come up with a good way to do this at this time.  Have entertained the thought of removing the current flange that holds the lugs and welding on another that would accomodate the larger 4.5, 4.75 or 5" pattern.  This has some OBVIOUS disadvantages.  I am looking at other race hubs to see if anything will work as is.  I need to find a good source of racing supplies to Europe and Asia for our metric sizes. <P>If that will not work, I am hoping that GM was lazy and our Fiero spindles might be close to something already out there.  Any variation on OD/ID's could be handled through the use of bearings.  I don't even know if this is possible, but again it has some serious DISADVANTAGES.  <P>Another option that I am looking into is having Street Dreams make some custom spindles for me.  They have indicated that they might be able to get some made up for little extra cost over the original price of the spindles.  This is the best solution as far as safety, etc, but is the worst in cost. <P>Unless some better alternatives make themselves available, I will most likely stick with the 5x100 pattern.  I will then have to fab a custom rotor adaptor to mate the 8 bolt racing rotor to the 5x100 pattern of the Fiero hub.   <P>If you have any positive suggestions, let me know.  <P>William T. Thorn, III <p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 11-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||bHooper||11-21-2000||11:15 PM||||WTIII.. how did youi find that place in Kernersville?  Have you talked to them on the phone?  Were they "fiero friendly"?<P>The reason I ask, is they are about 10 miles (or less) from my office.<P>what kind of place are they (performance shop?)?<P>Thanks!<BR>Hoop||||reg||5
Z||000033||BAM-BAM||11-21-2000||11:33 PM||||  Bshings, You say, you need bushings? <BR> <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/</A> <P> Here ya go, the best deal going.<P> Peace, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
Z||000034||william_thorniii||11-22-2000||08:36 AM||||hoop,<P>The place in Kernersville was recomended to me by Paul Vargyas.  Not sure if you know him, but he is a great guy.  <P><BR>BAM-BAM,<P>Here were some prices I was quoted by Suspension.com and Nopi<BR>                          Nopi    Suspension<BR>7-207 84-87 front SLA kit $32.37    $54.00<BR>7-501 dog bone bushings   $13.83    $20.00<BR>7-305 88 trailing link kit$48.98    $68.00<BR> - Those parts I ordered from Nopi<BR>9-5127 Sway Bar (not end link)      $14.00<BR>9-13101 Tie rod end boot            $4.00<BR> - Those parts Nopi doesn't even carry.  I did find the same tie rod end boot out there for $1 less elsewhere but ordered from suspension.com anyway.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||4
Z||000035||bHooper||11-22-2000||10:45 AM||||Yes, I am know of Paul.  I will have to make a trip to K-ville and check em out!||||reg||1
Z||000036||Will||11-22-2000||12:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>Will,<P>***<P>If that will not work, I am hoping that GM was lazy and our Fiero spindles might be close to something already out there.  Any variation on OD/ID's could be handled through the use of bearings.  I don't even know if this is possible, but again it has some serious DISADVANTAGES.  <P>***<P>Unless some better alternatives make themselves available, I will most likely stick with the 5x100 pattern.  I will then have to fab a custom rotor adaptor to mate the 8 bolt racing rotor to the 5x100 pattern of the Fiero hub.   <P>If you have any positive suggestions, let me know.  <P>William T. Thorn, III <P>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 11-21-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rotor Adapter? You're not using one piece rotors are you? By "one piece" I mean rotors like the OEM rear rotors on '84-87 cars.<BR>     I had just assumed that you were using rotors with hats. Do you mean "hat" when you say "rotor adapter"?<P>     I'll see what I can dig up on interchangable hubs for Fiero spindles.||||reg||1
Z||000037||william_thorniii||11-22-2000||02:17 PM||||Will,<P>No I am not planning on using one piece rotors.  I am planning on using any race ready 8 bolt rotor.  Head to <A HREF="http://www.wilwood.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wilwood.com</A>  they have an excellent exploded view of the entire system.<P>The rotor adaptor I am referring to is simply a cylindrical piece that mounts the rotor to the hub.  It has a thin cross section (near .375-.5") and has an I.D. small enough to accomodate the 100 bolt circle on the hub and an O.D. large enough to provide mounting holes for the rotors with an 8.?" hole pattern.  The wilwood site is a must for technical information.  They divulge all of their geometry.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||6
Z||000038||Will||11-22-2000||05:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>Will,<P>No I am not planning on using one piece rotors.  I am planning on using any race ready 8 bolt rotor.  Head to <A HREF="http://www.wilwood.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wilwood.com</A>  they have an excellent exploded view of the entire system.<P>***<P>William T. Thorn, III</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I guess we had little language barrier. I've always heard them called "hats" because they look like tophats.<BR>     When you said "rotor adapter" I was thinking of something like a wheel adapter to  put rotors with the big bolt circle on hubs with the small one.<BR>     It shoudln't be any problem at all to get a hat drilled with a 5x100 bolt circle.<BR>     And they really *are* called hats: <A HREF="http://www.wilwood.com/products/hats/hats.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wilwood.com/products/hats/hats.html</A>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000039||Will||11-26-2000||10:14 AM||||Have you seen Pontiaddict's latest in this thread? It has some ideas for changing the bolt circle to the 5 on 115 (?) used on F-bodies and S-10's.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004469.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004469.html</A> ||||reg||1
